 "about": "Golden cases for request-time synonym grouping and weights (the vocabulary miner's kept_weights + kept_groups), the spec civitai's P6 scorer ports; it reproduces the prototype's tag_weights -> gate_tags and tag_groups -> gate_groups on LEX (P = 1). Synthetic tags only. Step 1, 'groups': take the DISTINCT tag ids FOUND for the request (before the explicit gate); order them by descending idf, ties by key (code-point order); each tag joins the FIRST existing group (creation order) whose HEAD's near list contains it, else it starts a new group as its head; groups in creation order, each [head, members in joining order]. Step 2, 'keptWeights': every KEPT id's weight = idf of the HEAD of its step-1 group / the SIZE of its step-1 group (the gate never re-splits a weight); [id, weight] pairs by id, weights rounded to 9 decimals for comparison. Step 3, 'gatedGroups': drop every member not in keptIds and every group left empty, members keeping their order; then sort the groups by weight (the sum of the surviving members' weights, added in member order) DESCENDING, ties by the FIRST member's key. Grouping keptIds alone is NOT equivalent (case 'gated head holds two survivors together'). Every expected value was worked out by hand from the rule, not generated by the implementation. Shapes covered: idf ties broken by key, a chain k00~k01~k02 whose ends are NOT near, a tag with no near (k07), a tag near two heads (k11), a tag near a MEMBER but not its head (k05 near k04), duplicate ids, input order irrelevant, the empty request, a gated head whose survivors are not near each other (k12 near k13 and k14), a gated head with one survivor, a fully gated group, a gated member, a gated chain head (its weight stays 3.0/2 and its group sorts after a heavier one), and a weight tie broken by the first member's key where the last member's key would order it the other way (k15/k17 vs k16).",
